- Use directory handle to list all *.mp3 or *.mp4 files that have a matching *.txt
  and put them into the GUI: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemDirectoryHandle
- Song key display
- Semitone pitch adjustment during playback (adjust key)

Chord / lyrics demo

- Show sheet music or piano roll for vocals
- Show sheet music, piano roll, or tab for guitar
- Show blow pattern harmonica
- Show fingering for clarinet
- Show chord fingering/voicing for guitar
- Strumming motion for chords instead of bouncing ball (big/little down triangle, big/little up triangle, open circle, X)
- Horizontal scrolling mode (everything scrolls in from right)
- Singer: show vocal trainer
- Guitarist: show rhythm, solo, or lead trainer
- Drop into rhythm trainer / jam

Library server
==============

Problem: reaching local disk from the web UI is clunky (directory handles,
re-granting permission, nothing persists, no way for a phone on a music stand
to see the same files). Fix: run a small local server over the music folder and
have every client - web, native, terminal - speak to it instead of the disk.

- one server, three clients. The protocol is the only thing they share; nothing
  client-side needs a filesystem. A phone at the far end of the room and the
  laptop driving the PA both hit the same URL.
- filesystem stays the source of truth. song.mp3 + song.txt sidecars as now;
  the server parses and normalises. An index (sqlite or a json cache) is a
  derived artifact that can always be rebuilt by rescanning. No lock-in, no
  import step, files stay editable by hand.
- stable ids from content hash, not path, so charts survive renames and moves.

- endpoints, roughly:
    GET  /library                list: id, title, artist, secs, tempo, key,
                                 which sidecars exist
    GET  /song/{id}              metadata + chart + lyric timing
    GET  /audio/{id}             media, must support Range for seeking
    GET  /art/{id}
    PUT  /song/{id}/chart        beatmapper and hand edits write back
    GET  /search?q=
    GET  /setlist  PUT /setlist/{id}
    GET  /events                 SSE: file added/changed, analysis finished

- offer line-oriented variants of the read endpoints (/library.tsv) beside the
  json. A terminal or embedded client should not need a json parser to list
  songs - one split per line and it is done. Cheap to serve, cheap to consume.

- analysis belongs on the server, not the client. Beat/key detection and stem
  separation are exactly what a browser cannot do and a local machine can:
    POST /analyze/{id}           -> job id
    GET  /job/{id}               -> progress, then result written as a sidecar
  Long jobs need progress reporting, and results are cached as files so a
  rescan doesn't redo them.

- watch the directory and push changes over /events, so the library updates
  live while you're editing charts in another window.

- discovery: advertise _riffhound._tcp over mDNS so clients find the server
  without anyone typing an IP. This is the same lookup the jam-along QR code
  wants - joining a jam and finding the library are the same problem.

- auth: nothing on localhost. A PIN or token for LAN, since "everyone in the
  room" is the threat model, not the internet.

- the jam session is the same server. Song, position, tempo, key and section
  marks are session state; the library is what you pick from. One connection,
  and the host is whoever is already serving the music.

Native + terminal clients
=========================

The web stack is too heavy for old or small machines, and a fast native UI
would be nice regardless. See textui/chords for the VT100 sketch.

- keep the wire format small enough that a client with no json parser and no
  audio decoder is still useful: tsv endpoints, and a way to ask the server for
  decoded PCM or just let it drive playback locally.
- a terminal client that only renders chart + position (server plays the audio)
  is a genuinely small program and covers the music-stand case.
